   "FavorStory_121203_Title": "Chapter III: Dream of No Return",
   "FavorStory_121204_Content": "The marketplace was always lively in the afternoons, stalls of every kind lining the streets. An old candy maker slowly stirred a pot of syrup, golden strands trailing from his ladle in fine, gleaming arcs. Steam billowed from the bun shop nearby, carrying the rich smell of meat and chili far down the road. In an open square, a mechanical stage had drawn a crowd: a troupe of Autopuppets tumbled and danced to the beat of the drums, earning burst after burst of applause from the onlookers.\n\nJingran stopped by a fabric shop to pick out a bolt of moon-white cloth for Yanshu, then bought Nian a mechanical jade rabbit that could hop about on its own. As he paid, the shopkeeper smiled and complimented his taste. But Jingran wasn't nearly so confident. Picking gifts for his Shifu's old lady and his little sister had always proven far more troublesome than venturing into any Mirage Realm. Not long ago, he'd brought Nian a mechanical pellet drum, two round-faced lucky children painted on either side. Spin it, and the little figures would bob their heads and unfurl a tiny scroll that read \"Good Fortune and Prosperity.\" Jingran had thought the thing was a stroke of genius. Nian, however, crossed her arms as he showed her how it worked, then heaved a world-weary sigh. \"Bubba, I'm really too old for this.\"\n\nThe pellet drum ended up back in Jingran's hands. That evening, he fiddled with it late into the night, quietly wondering what on earth was wrong with it. That question still haunts him.\n\nWith that memory still fresh, he bought the jade rabbit without a second thought. Nian will definitely like this one. Before leaving, he made one last stop at a pastry shop for a Little Snowy Moon—layers of snow-white cake stacked neatly into the shape of a full moon, as if sculpted from fresh-fallen snow. The shopkeeper smiled and asked if they were for his family. Jingran didn't answer. He simply took the paper-wrapped parcel and ambled off toward the river.\n\nIt was the day Qiuyuan left <te href=850154>Mengzhou</te>.\n\nA strong wind swept across the water at the dock, setting the flecks of gold dancing on the surface. The passenger ship had nearly finished casting off.\n\nJingran spotted him from a distance, a lone figure in black standing beside the ship, robes snapping sharply in the wind. As always, he carried the unmistakable air of a man best left undisturbed. Jingran found his thoughts drifting back to stories from years ago.\n\nWhenever his master had one drink too many, he would bring up a certain young swordsman. A once-in-a-generation talent, he'd claim. The praise had stoked Jingran's curiosity for years. Now he had finally met the man in the flesh. Unfortunately, he still hadn't had the chance to test himself against that sword the man's holding.\n\nQiuyuan noticed him too, and dipped his head in greeting.\n\nJingran waved and strolled over. Stopping at the railing, he glanced out at the river before drawling, \"Got everything packed away?\"\n\nQiuyuan knew he meant the modular part recovered from Muyu. He nodded. But before he could answer, Jingran raised a hand.\n\n\"Save it.\"\n\n\"I couldn't care less about the old dramas and squabbles of <te href=260009>Mingting</te>. What I need to know, I already know. What I don't, I've no interest in knowing. So spare me the explanation.\"\n\nAnother gust swept across the water, scattering the reflections into rippling fragments.\n\n\"That said,\" Jingran went on, turning to him with a raised brow, \"I did help you get what you came for. I'm putting that one on your tab.\"\n\nQiuyuan tilted his head slightly, those deep gray eyes as unreadable as ever.\n\n\"What do you want?\"\n\n\"Haven't decided yet.\"\n\nJingran grinned.\n\n\"When I think of something, I'll come collect.\"\n\nSilence settled between them for a moment.\n\nAt last, Qiuyuan nodded.\n\n\"Very well.\"\n\nSatisfied, Jingran pushed the paper-wrapped parcel he'd been carrying into the other man's arms.\n\n\"Here. A parting gift.\"\n\nThe river wind tugged at their sleeves. In the distance, the boatman began calling passengers aboard, and the dock grew busier. Qiuyuan tucked the parcel away and turned toward the ship. After a few steps, he stopped.\n\n\"Jingran.\"\n\n\"Hm?\"\n\n\"Offer a stick of incense to your Shifu for me.\"\n\nJingran went still. Then, after a moment, he smiled.\n\n\"Sure.\"\n\nThe ferry pulled slowly away. Water lapped against the hull, sending rings of ripples spreading across the river. Little by little the vessel drifted into the evening mist, until it melted into the dusk. Jingran stayed where he was, watching until the silhouettes of man and ship had vanished into the gilded horizon.\n\nThe wind stirred the reeds along the riverbank.\n\nAt that very moment, he remembered another evening, many years ago. His Shifu had been sitting outside the courtyard gate, shelling peanuts as he talked about that young swordsman. Every time the story reached a good part, the old man would laugh and clap him on the shoulder.\n\n\"You're not half bad yourself, brat. Give it a few more years, and I'll take you to Chongzhou to cross blades with him in person.\"\n\nBack then, Jingran had had little idea what Chongzhou was really like. He'd simply taken his Shifu at his word and gazed off toward the horizon beyond Mengzhou. Dusk had been spilling over the mountain ridges from the far side. Birds crossed the sky overhead, beating their wings toward lands farther still.\n\nJingran lowered his head and chuckled softly. Then he turned and started for home.\n\nThe sun had already sunk behind the mountains, leaving only a thin ribbon of molten gold along the horizon.\n\nOne by one, lanterns lit up the length of the street, their warm glow stretching on and on down the limestone road.\n\nAnd by that light, Jingran found the road that led home.",
