-  "FavorStory_121205_Content": "The teahouse was always busy at noon.\n\nMore than a dozen tables filled the main hall. Waiters wove through the crowd with brass kettles in hand. The fragrance of tea mingled with the scent of wine and the hum of conversation, lingering. By a window on the second floor sat a young man, chin propped on one hand as he lazily tossed peanuts into his mouth with the other. Downstairs, the storyteller had reached the most thrilling part of his tale, his voice rising above the din of the hall and drifting steadily upward.\n\n\"Just then, the ancient ebony ship came bursting out of the mist. Three hundred lanterns blazed from its prow, their light falling upon a forgotten sea untouched for a thousand years. Shadows crowded the deck, yet every face was deathly pale, as though not a soul among them belonged to the living. And when one man finally stepped aboard for a closer look...\"\n\nThe storyteller brought down his wooden clapper with a crack. The hall fell silent, everyone holding their breath for what came next.\n\nCalmly, the storyteller lifted his teacup and took a sip.\n\n\"As for what happened next... that will have to wait for the next chapter.\"\n\nFor a heartbeat the teahouse went dead quiet, then the crowd erupted.\n\nThe storyteller gave a helpless sigh.\n\n\"My friends, it isn't that I won't go on. This is simply where 'Mirage Seeker's Tales' left off...\"\n\nThat only made the grumbling louder.\n\n\"You always stop at the best part!\"\n\n\"Hasn't this chapter been held up for half a month already?\"\n\n\"Half a month?\" someone scoffed, slapping the table. \"The Cat of the Nether Lamp once went missing for three whole months!\"\n\nJingran nearly choked on his peanut. He quietly turned his gaze to the window and did his best to look like none of this had anything to do with him. Unfortunately, at that very moment, the chair across from him scraped back.\n\n\"So this is where the culprit's been hiding.\"\n\nA man in a teal robe settled into the seat across from him, a thick stack of manuscripts under his arm.\n\nIt was Shouci, the editor in charge of proofreading and publishing \"Mirage Seeker's Tales.\" Jingran looked up and flashed a grin.\n\n\"Perfect timing.\" He waved him over. \"This meal's on me.\"\n\n\"Whenever you're this generous, something's wrong.\" Shouci dropped the stack of manuscripts onto the table with a thump. \"How much of Volume Two have you actually written?\"\n\nThe smile vanished from Jingran's face at once. He slumped onto the table as though every bone in his body had dissolved, burying his chin in his folded arms. \"No inspiration...\"\n\n\"Spare me.\"\n\n\"I mean it.\" Jingran let out a long sigh. \"Mirage Realms, ancient ruins, forgotten relics... I write about the same things day after day. Even I'm getting sick of reading it.\"\n\nShouci raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"So?\"\n\n\"So I'm thinking of having a new protagonist.\"\n\nThat caught Shouci off guard.\n\nJingran began ticking points off on his fingers. \"The first volume's nearly wrapped up anyway. Might as well shake things up. It can't always be me hauling a lamp around and getting into trouble.\"\n\n\"And just what sort of person could possibly be your new protagonist?\"\n\nJingran thought for a moment. \"Someone hard to read.\"\n\nPropping his chin on one hand, he gazed out the window.\n\n\"Have you ever met someone like that? Steady and restrained, clear-headed and dependable. While everyone else is still weighing the pros and cons, {Male=he;Female=she}'s already decided which road to take. And while everyone else rushes to make choices for other people, {Male=he;Female=she} always leaves the choice to them.\"\n\nThe more Shouci listened, the more far-fetched it sounded. \"And where do you find a person like that? You're describing a god in a shrine.\"\n\nBefore Jingran could argue, his gaze drifted outside.\n\nThe long street was bustling under the midday sun. People streamed endlessly along the thoroughfare, while sunlight spilled over the rooftops and draped the street below in a veil of gold.\n\nThrough the crowd, a familiar figure came toward him from the far end of the road. Jingran blinked. Then his eyes curved into a smile.\n\n\"{PlayerName}!\"\n\nHe called out without a shred of restraint, his voice carrying clear across the street. The figure stopped and instinctively looked up.\n\nSunlight caught those golden eyes just then, and they glittered so brightly it was hard to look away.\n\nJingran waved through the window, then turned to Shouci with a triumphant smirk.\n\n\"See? In the flesh.\"\n\nShouci was left speechless.\n\nUnfortunately, that shout had also caught the attention of everyone else in the teahouse. The guests turned toward the sound, up at the second floor. A few squinted for a better look. Then, all of a sudden, someone slapped the table and cried out.\n\n\"Hold on—\"\n\n\"Isn't that the Cat of the Nether Lamp up there?!\"\n\nThe teahouse froze for a beat. The next instant, dozens of eyes shot upward as one. Jingran slowly lowered the pastry in his hand.\n\n\"...\"\n\n\"Master?!\"\n\n\"It really is him!\"\n\n\"Finally caught you!\"\n\n\"What was inside the ebony ship?!\"\n\n\"When's Volume Two coming out?!\"\n\nThe hall exploded into chaos. Chairs scraped across the floor as guests scrambled to their feet. Even the storyteller craned his neck to peer upstairs.\n\nSensing that the whole crowd was about to descend on him from every direction, Jingran closed his eyes with an air of I knew this would happen.\n\n\"Shouci,\" He snatched the manuscripts off the table. \"See you around.\"\n\nThen, amid a chorus of startled cries, he stepped up onto the windowsill and dropped. The second floor stood a fair height above the street. To the guests nearby, he seemed to vanish in the blink of an eye. His figure sailing over the railing, graceful as a bird, robes billowing in the wind. The next moment, he had already touched down lightly on the street below.\n\n\"He got away!\" someone yelled.\n\nThe teahouse plunged into even greater bedlam. Footsteps and shouts thundered through the hall. But Jingran merely dusted off his sleeves as if nothing had happened. A few easy strides carried him through the crowd, and he came to a stop in front of {PlayerName}.\n\nHe glanced first at the swelling mob of pursuers gathering behind him, then at the person before him, and raised a brow with a smile.\n\n\"Perfect timing.\"\n\n\"Come on. Another minute and they really would've dragged me off to finish the next chapter.\" He jerked a thumb toward the commotion behind him.\n\n\"Let's get out of here—together. My treat.\"",
