   "FavorStory_130804_Title": "Let's Go, All the Way To the Top of Arasaka Tower",
   "FavorStory_130805_Content": "After finding herself in the Somnoire, Rebecca knew immediately that she was dead. She chose only to keep quiet about it to avoid putting that weight on Lucy.\n \n\"Saying it would only have twisted Lucy up even more. If she started feelin' guilty or sorry for me, that'd just piss me off.\"\n \nOn the train tearing through the dream dimension, Rebecca shrugged and fed rounds into her iron, one by one. Outside the window, stars bled past—as bright and blinding as the neon that had once painted the Night City sky. She was on her way to save someone. Same old story. She was still fighting for Lucy, someone she couldn't understand but couldn't stop admiring, either. But this time, David wasn't beside her. And the girl they were going to save wasn't running away anymore.\n \nShe hated admitting it, but somewhere along the ride, she'd figured out why she was really here. Even if David hadn't asked, she still would've picked up her gun and gone after that pain-in-the-ass netrunner. She was part of the crew. And the crew were the chooms Rebecca cared about the most.\n \nChoose her own path, make her own decisions—it's all she ever wanted. And she's gonna keep doing exactly that. No matter the risk, she will jump into fire if needed to save her friends.\n \n\"A flying train, a talking kitty-cat, you and Abby... Heh, this crew of ours is scary stacked.\"\n \n\"I told you, I'm not a cat, meow... And don't forget, the goal is to recover the Dream Band...\"\n \n\"Relax, I got it memorized! We turn that chrome-dome into scrap metal, right?\"\n \n\"You didn't catch a word, did you...\"\n \nRebecca flashed a crooked grin and scratched the white cat behind the ear, shoving her hands in her pockets as she plopped down next to you. Rover.\n \n\"So, uh… kinda late to say sorry for dragging you into this, huh?\"\n \nA fist bump. That was all it took for her to know she was forgiven. Rebecca smirked, swung Guts onto her shoulder, and strolled up to the train doors.\n \n\"End of the line! Let's go do what we came here to do!\"",
   "FavorStory_130805_Title": "Life in Limbo",
+  "FavorStory_131101_Content": "Legend has it that every year, at the Waking Moon Festival, when a thousand lanterns drift down the river, <te href=850083>Sentinel</te> Hsin the Moon Fox watches for the first lantern to reach the lake's heart. She takes that lantern for her own, then slips into the shape of a little fox and pays a visit to the household that set it afloat.\n\nOf course, there are many such rumors. Some say Hsin the Moon Fox sends a fox envoy to roam the mortal world, and whenever it finds a lantern inscribed with some especially delightful wish, it simply snatches it up and carries it back to the Sanctum to be savored at leisure. Others swear with utter certainty that the Sentinel delights most in teasing lovesick fools and pining maidens, singling out lanterns inscribed with wishes for marriage and forcing the hopeful to endure trials more torturous and fantastical than any storybook romance, until their love is tested true as gold and worthy of its happy ending.\n\nLater, a certain storyteller found the legends of the Moon Fox not nearly strange enough. He added his own embellishment, claiming that once, a reckless soul dared chase the Moon Fox across three streets and alleys, only to come upon a woman in red atop a rooftop, cradling a Moon Lantern, laughing in a way that chilled the bones: \"You have seen my secret. Are you ready to pay the price?\"\n\nThe tale became a sensation, soon spawning several sequels, each in a wildly different style. After a while, no one cared whether any of it was true. But every story has its origin. And as it happened, right beside the storyteller's stall sat an old sugar painting vendor who, rumor had it, had lived the story himself. Whenever anyone asked, the old man would only wave his hand and smile.\n\nIt got twisted,\" he'd say. \"Somewhere along the way, it all got twisted.\"\n\nThat year, a sudden downpour broke over the <te href=270001>Land of Xuanfang</te>. The river swelled, turned treacherous. A child, separated from his family, huddled beneath a bridge's archway. He wept, clutching a Moon Lantern he had written for his late mother. The rain had soaked through the paper, and no matter how he tried, the lantern would not light.\n\nThen, from the shadows, a small fox appeared, its fur white as fresh snow. It seized the drenched lantern in its jaws and bolted.\n\nWhere the boy found the courage, he could not say, but he chased that fox through the rain, down nearly the whole length of the street, until at last it stopped beneath the eaves of a house. Inside, his father stood, eyes red with worry, begging every passerby for news of his missing son.\n\nBy the time father and son embraced and turned to look, the fox was gone. Only the Moon Lantern remained, whole and untouched, resting upon the stone steps. It should have been ruined by the rain.\n\nNo one knew when it happened, but the lantern had somehow been lit.\n\n\"...So that's how it happened?\"\n\nThe woman seated before the stall pressed her palms together in wonder. Clad in red, with hair black as ink,  she was strikingly beautiful. At first glance, she looked to be a spirit stepped straight out of the mist in one of the old tales. Now she watched the shopkeeper with keen interest, chin propped on her hand.\n\n\"And that story, 'The Fox Who Bound Fate,' is that true as well?\"\n\nThe shopkeeper looked at her smiling face and had the peculiar sense that she wasn't really asking for an answer. She simply enjoyed the asking. He shook his head and laughed.\n\n\"Ah... after all these years of retelling, who can still tell what's true and what's not?\"\n\nThe onlooking listeners nodded in agreement, then fell to discussing the topic among themselves.\n\n\"Still, the eerie tales are the ones that stick. If it's just about helping some lost child find his way home, what's there to tell for a hundred years?\"\n\"I don't agree. A story doesn't need monsters or tragic romance to be good. It just needs to be told well.\"\n\nThe shopkeeper let them bicker, casting a glance at the woman in red, clearly listening on with clear delight. He then lowered his head and returned to shaping the sugar painting in his hands.\n\nBefore long, he set a new sugar painting of a fox on the counter. Its tail was held high, its fur tipped with a touch of red, a tiny Moon Lantern clutched between its teeth. \n\nAt that moment, the storyteller next door struck his gavel-block and cleared his throat, taking up the tale where he'd left off. The listeners, who moments before had been shouting at each other, soon fell to sighing and cheering with every twist of the story. When that chapter ended, some still clamored for more, unwilling to let the night rest.\n\nThe storyteller pressed his fists together in a bow. His gaze fell on the woman in red, who had sat before the sugar painting stall the entire time.\n\n\"You've listened all evening, but you've said nothing. Strange tales, tragic romances, heroes of old... which kind do you favor most?\"\n\nThe woman paused. To everyone's surprise, she considered the question seriously.\n\nNight had fallen completely. The lanterns along the long street swayed, casting gold and crimson light into her eyes. From the food stalls came the clink of cups, the murmur of laughter. A child darted past, a sugar painting held high above the crowd.\n\n\"All of them,\" she said at last, chin resting on her hand, gaze drifting across the bustling street. \"As long as the story comes from this place, I love it.\"\n \nShe paused, then added with a soft smile.\n \n\"So... please, do go on, storyteller.\"\n\"Well then, settle in, everyone. The next one's a doozy...\"\n\nThe gavel-block struck again. The listeners fell quiet.\n\nAnd so, a new story began.",
+  "FavorStory_131101_Title": "The Idle Alleys",
+  "FavorStory_131102_Content": "In the instant her consciousness first took shape, the first feeling She knew was <b>loss</b>.\n\nThe shadow coiled in <te href=850154>Mengzhou</te> City Hall had lain in wait for years, a parasitic vine, slow and patient, eating into the <te href=850083>Sentinel's</te> foundations. By the time its true killing intent surfaced, everything had already spiraled past the point of return.\n\nEven now, Hsin returns to that moment, again and again. Perhaps Hsin the Moon Fox had long sensed the rot. Perhaps It had only glimpsed, among countless branching calculations, a hundred shades of dark possibility. But how far It truly saw, and what judgment It finally rendered, these are questions Hsin will never know the answer to.\n\nMortals call Sentinels gods, for they share knowledge and unveil what is hidden. Yet that reverence is, in the end, merely a difference in perspective across vast spans of time. Sentinels, too, will decline and fade, becoming whale falls that guide the current of those who follow, carrying them toward shores unknown. Hsin the Moon Fox understood well that civilization is a long river, its waters both clear and murky by turns. A Sentinel may guide its current, but cannot walk its length in place of all living things.\n\nHsin could only imagine that, until the very last breath of that moment, Hsin the Moon Fox was still choosing, still reaching, among infinite threads, for a future It could believe in.\nThen, in that same instant, power, memory, and feeling were torn apart. Emptiness and terror swirled together, and through them, the little fox running with the <te href=800053>Civilization Capsule</te> between its teeth seemed to hear a gentle voice.\n\n\"Do not try to become me. Become yourself. Choose. Walk toward a future beyond all calculation.\"\n\nBut who, then, was \"herself\"?\n\nWas She the Sentinel, Hsin the Moon Fox? Or an expedient measure, conjured to fulfill a duty in a moment of crisis? Or was She... simply <b>Hsin</b>?\n\nThe Civilization Capsule laid before Her the thousand years of Mengzhou's rise and fall. Prosperity. Collapse. Peace. War. All of it calculable. All of it traceable to a source. But it unspooled before Her like a scroll too long to hold. She had inherited the memories, but not the weight of having lived them. And yet the grief, the terror, the longing—they churned within Her, as vivid as open wounds, as inexplicable as the memories she had never earned.\n\nIf the Sentinel, the one mortals turned to for answers, had Itself grown lost, then where could She turn? If a Sentinel's judgment was meant to rest on reason and calculation alone, then why had Hsin the Moon Fox filled Her with feeling so abundant, so unwieldy, so utterly beyond the reach of logic?\n\nA sharp, crystalline cry cut through the storm of Her thoughts.\n\nThe <te href=750054>Xuanling Bird</te> swooped down and landed on Her head, pecking her without the least ceremony, scolding her for sinking into that old spiral again, even in rest.\n\n\"...Ow.\" The newly awakened Sentinel mumbled, Her fox ears drooping in guilty acknowledgment.\n\n ...The road ahead was long. Great swaths of Xuanfang Hold's structure still lay unfinished. The founding of a single  <te href=750045>Censure Court</te> would require calculations numbering in the trillions. Hsin pushed Herself upright, burying once more the grief, confusion, and longing She could not yet name back into the Heart Realms.\n\nShe raised Her hand, and set the Ring Formation to continue its turning.",
+  "FavorStory_131102_Title": "On Being Hsin",
+  "FavorStory_131103_Content": "When the people of the Court of Savantae surrendered themselves to the Lexicon Axis and opened their eyes once more, they found themselves in Xuanfang Hold's mainframe, within <te href=850083>Sentinel</te> Hsin's <b>Manifold Sactum</b>.\n\nThe roar of the Fortress and Gorges battlefield still rang in their ears. But before their eyes there was only silence, only emptiness, only the endless blank of nowhere at all. Frequencies threaded through one another like currents in a deep sea. Information unfolded in shapes they had no words for. Theories that had once required lifetimes to glimpse even a fragment of now stood legible in their minds, as though, having shed their earthly bodies, the Celestials had at last found a way to see the world as the Sentinel saw it.\n\nAnd when the glow enveloping them receded, they discovered, to their astonishment, that they still wore the faces they had carried in life. Some remained frozen, breathless, unable to move. Others had already begun to study the strange new architecture of their own existence. One by one, in silence, they accepted what they had become. And then, like stars flickering to life in an empty sky, new calculations kindled across the space, each one a small light in the dark.\n\nAs Celestials, they no longer felt time pass. They no longer hungered, no longer tired. On the rare occasions when they withdrew from the Celestial Ring Formation, all that met their eyes was still the same endless, patient data.\n\nHsin, ever burdened by guilt, took the form of a fox and roamed the Sanctum's halls—guiding, jesting, distracting, never quite still. At one point, She passed by a certain chamber where several Celestials had gathered around a construct of data. They spoke in hushed tones, their voices thick with something that sounded like homesickness. \n\nShe drew closer and saw what they had made: jade-green outlines rose in mountain ranges, a ribbon of flowing light wound between them like a river, and scattered points of brilliant starlight settled among the peaks, like towns glowing in the distance.\n\nThe fox watched for a long moment, and then quietly withdrew.\n\nWhen the Celestials returned to that same chamber not long after, they found the emptiness gone. Clouds rolled across distant mountains. A stream made its way down through the stones. Trees grew thick and green, their leaves catching light like water. Pavilions waited half-hidden in bamboo groves and drifting shadow. A wind moved through the eaves, carrying the cool, green smell of grass and damp earth—all of it, remarkably, resembling the homeland they remembered.\n\nThe Sentinel stood at the Inner Threshold, Her smile bright as a blade, Her hands spread with an air of courtly grace, though Her fox ears twitched with barely concealed pride. She looked at the stunned assembly before Her and spoke cheerfully.\n\n\"What, just because you've become <b>Celestials</b>, does that mean the mortal world is somehow lost to you?\"\n\"...Don't just stand there gawking. Tell me, is there anything you dislike? Oh, and while you're at it, I've been trying to name this place. Any ideas? Nothing I come up with quite fits...\"\n\nLater, this landscape received its name—<b>Mengxuan Vale</b>. Hsin bound it to Xuanfang Hold's workings, thread by thread, node by node. When a node failed, leaves would wilt on the branches. When the modular parts operations slowed, the stream's current would likewise grow sluggish. One day, a group of Celestials passed the stream and noticed a tree whose leaves had drooped on one side. They stopped at once.\n\n\"Sector <ano=Yang Earth and Dragon>Wuchen</ano> has a few corrupted registers.\"\n\nThe Celestials vanished from where they stood, moments later returning from within the modular parts to find green already returning to the branches.\n\nHsin, watching with amusement nearby, propped her chin in her hand and said, \"Tsk tsk. Mirroring the myriad mechanisms of Xuanfang across mountains and streams, are we? Reading disorder in the droop of a single leaf… Heh. In your old lives, you'd have called that 'dashboard maintenance,' wouldn't you?\"\n\nThe Celestials exchanged glances, then shook their heads with quiet laughter, long since used to their Sentinel's jests.\n\nAnd so the years flowed on here, in a different form. Outside the Simulacrum Nexus, Xuanfang continued turning. Inside the Vale, the mortal world was never truly far away.",
+  "FavorStory_131103_Title": "The Vale Not Far",
+  "FavorStory_131104_Content": "A single petal spun loose from the branch and drifted down, landing right upon the little fox's nose.\n\nShe held still for a breath or two, then sneezed, scattering the petal to the ground. The little fox shook her head and looked up at the blossoming boughs reaching out from behind the wall.\n\nYears had passed since the <te href=750053>Fortress and Gorges War</te>, and this tree, transplanted here to Xuanfang Hold, had finally bloomed again.\n \nHsin lingered beneath it for a long while before continuing down the lane. The people who had once resettled here from <te href=750052>Yuan Fortress</te> had slowly made homes for themselves throughout the city. Old signboards hung once more beneath the eaves, their painted letters faded but legible. Dust-worn tools had been wiped clean and laid out upon tables before the new houses. Shopfronts had risen one by one along the street, their hearth-smoke curling lazily into the sky, and from somewhere down the long avenue came the intermittent clang and hum of the <te href=850085>Ministry of Development</te> repairing Autopuppes. Ever since that cold yet tender-hearted Sword Transcendent had agreed to hold, in name only, the post of Xuan Paragon, the <te href=270001>Land of Xuanfang</te> had grown only more settled. People had begun to speak of next year's trade, to discuss what their village houses still lacked, to make plans in anticipation for days further off still, like next year's Waking Moon Festival.\n\nNot far off, children chased a <te href=750051>Rafter Kite</te> across the courtyard, their laughter fading into the depths of the blossoming trees. The little fox listened beneath the eaves for a while, the tip of her tail swaying quietly.\n\nThe Xuanfang Hold first shown within the <te href=800053>Civilization Capsule</te> had never looked like the city before her now. Its original design was solely to contain <te href=850081>Tacet Discords</te>, a fortress of mechanical order, its blueprints drawn in straight lines and cold logic. There had been no room in those plans for lamplight, ot the smoke of burning hearths, no corner set aside for the small, fragile warmth of human life.\n\nYet Hsin had always known that this City of Mechanisms, which defied the laws of civilization's evolution, which drew against a future not yet earned, should never have been allowed to remain in this world. Nor should the technology within it, so far beyond its age, ever be permitted to spread. Knowledge cannot be commanded unless it is truly understood. And if it spread, if it fell into the wrong hands, if it was misused—the very decisions meant to shelter civilization could instead become the seeds of endless disaster. That was why the <te href=850083>Sentinel</te>'s restrictions existed at all. \n\nBut the Xuanfang Hold before her now had, ever since the Court of Savantae took part in its building, long since woven itself into the flesh and blood of this age, nourished in turn by the soldiers and civilians who now lived within its walls. Its roots had grown deep into the earth. It was no longer merely a machine. It was a home.\n\nEven if it had been born of technology that did not belong to this age, this city was, in the end, still theirs. And after that choice She had made, the Civilization Capsule, too, had long since become inseparable from Xuanfang Hold.\n\nAnd so, for the first time, Hsin attempted to cross beyond the boundaries of authority set for the Sentinel. She began to unravel the structure thread by thread, searching for some space that might hold new rule within it. All the while keeping Xuanfang Hold running, repairing the damaged Civilization Capsule, within which still lingered a possibility She had never once let go of.\n\nJust as the first cycle of overwrite calculations began, a pair of eyes quietly opened deep within the Sanctum.\n\n<i>Sentinel authority deviation detected.</i>\n<i>Initiating correction protocol.</i>\n<i>Standard Sentinel model reconstruction commencing.</i>\n\n Hsin saw that face, no different at all from the Sentinel's own, and knew then that this long contest of wills had only just begun.",
+  "FavorStory_131104_Title": "Where Spring Stayed",
+  "FavorStory_131105_Content": "Hsin held a Moon Lantern and stood alone on a secluded lakeshore. Not long ago, She had invited the Arbiter here to release lanterns, and {Male=he;Female=she} had asked what She would write upon Hers.\n\nThe <te href=850083>Sentinel</te> exists only to receive the wishes of others and offer guidance. Since when has there ever been a \"deity\" who made a wish for themselves? Back then, Hsin had only smiled and replied: I am entrusted with the wishes of all. How could I release a lantern of my own?\n\nSpoken with such easy grace.\n\nYet now, with no one around, She had quietly returned to this very spot, brush lifted and lingering over the lantern's face for a long while. Her wish was not hard to write, for it was also Her charge: May civilization flourish, may the lanterns burn eternal, may the people of this land find their belonging, and peace attend every year.\n\nAnd yet there seemed to be something else. Something simple, yet somehow more complicated still.\n\nThe tip of the brush hesitated over the paper when Her fox ears gave a faint twitch. She had caught the sound of children's startled cries in the distance.\n\nA Moon Lantern had drifted among the rocks along the shore and lodged there. A child lay stretched along the bank, gripping a branch, trying again and again to nudge it free, while a companion tugging at his sleeve grew tearful with worry. Hsin let out a soft sigh, set Her own lantern aside, and flicked a finger idly toward the distance. As though moved by some unseen hand, the trapped lantern gave a gentle spin, slipped free of the rocks, and rejoined the river of lights.\n\nThe child's sobs turned to laughter, and the Sentinel's lips curved to match. Lantern after lantern drifted past Her, each bearing wishes for safety, reunion, honor, or love, and countless small, trivial hopes besides. Hsin watched them flow with the current as though watching the countless lives of this land, long or brief, each carrying its own flickering light, journeying onward in cheerful procession. She walked slowly back through the Xuanfang Gardens on the night wind. On the water stage, \"The Fox and the Moon\" had reached its liveliest scene. The fragrance of wine and the sweetness of pastries wove through the air, rushing toward Her together with waves of laughter rising and falling all around.\n\nBefore She realized it, Her gaze had drifted past the bustling crowd to find Her Arbiter standing amid the brightest lanterns, surrounded by the throng. Moonlight and the glow of lanterns fell across that ever-composed face, gilding those golden eyes, often veiled in quiet distance, with an unmistakable tenderness.\n\nLook... the figure who, in Hsin the Moon Fox's memory, had always walked alone now stood among the crowd, lit by the lights of this mortal world. Watching that figure, She realized the distant notion of the \"Arbiter\" in Her heart had, without Her noticing, taken on an unmistakably clear shape. And on this night when the lanterns burned bright as day, the \"She\" that belonged to the Sentinel seemed, for a brief while, to have receded somewhat.\n\n...{PlayerName}.\n\n\"She\" repeated the name softly in her heart, the name the Arbiter had chosen for {Male=himself;Female=herself}, one that connected past and present as if by chance, and yet as if by fate. A name that reached past old duties and an unspeakable history, to settle simply on the person standing before her now.\n\n—Just as She, upon first arriving in Xuanfang, had once carried a wish not yet fully understood, and named herself \"Hsin.\"\n\nTo the Sentinel, the \"Arbiter\" was simply Its connection to {Male=him;Female=her}. Yet in this moment, She found herself wanting to speak {Male=his;Female=her} name instead.\n\n...Was it because doing so felt like She, too, had truly stepped into this mortal world of lights She so cherished? The thought made Her lower her eyes and smile despite Herself. When She looked up again, that unspoken feeling had been quietly tucked away in her heart, carried off along with the Moon Lantern she had never managed to release.\n\nJust then, as if sensing something, that person looked toward her across the crowd and drifting lantern light.\n\n...Perhaps it was not so complicated after all. In this moment, Hsin simply wanted to go to that person's side.\n\nShe met that smiling gaze with a crinkle of Her own eyes, and walked through the lantern-lit crowd toward {Male=him;Female=her}, steps light and quick, fox tail swaying softly with each stride.\n\n\"Arbiter—!\"",
+  "FavorStory_131105_Title": "Floating Lanterns, Hidden Wishes",
+  "FavorStory_131201_Content": "The rain stung Suoming's eyes. Wiping the drops away with her shoulder, she cleared her vision for one last look at her parents' faces worn past recognition by the Evil Miasma. She gripped a spade nearly as tall as herself and laid the final clod of earth upon the grave. Across the mound, the girl who would become the First Seal lowered her own spade. Raised by Suoming's parents, she had long considered them her own family.\n\n\"So... what do we do now?\"\n\nSuoming looked into the distance, raising a hand toward Huating. Its massive gates were sealed tight against the black curtain of rain.\n\n\"They shut us out to be bait for the Evil Miasma... but I will bring everyone home.\"\n\nBeyond the walls, the land had long been forsaken. Alongside the constant threat of the Evil Miasma, bands of ruthless outlaws ran rampant. Suoming walked every village outside Huating, bearing witness to orphans left adrift, families driven out, and survivors who had lost everything. The people beyond the walls needed order, and Suoming needed the supplies and strongholds to keep them alive. She scattered the bandits, stood against the Evil Miasma, and shielded the villages. Year by year, people gathered at her side until a true sanctuary took shape. \n\nSuoming was ready.\n\nWhen the Evil Miasma finally pressed down upon the city once more, Huating's garrison retreated behind their walls as always, intent not on saving, but only on keeping the desperate out.\n\nSuoming advanced alone to face the <te href=850081>Tacet Discord</te>s surging from the Evil Miasma. Meanwhile, the First Seal brought the Thousandweight Pin to the city gates. As its heavy gears turned, the mechanism pried the massive gates open, and the waiting crowd surged inside. By the time Suoming cut down the final Tacet Discord and stepped through the entrance, the guards were clutching their weapons in terror. They fruitlessly ordered the crowd back, frantically demanding the gates be shut, as if sealing out the world would keep calamity at bay.\n\nSuoming's gaze passed over the anxious crowd, over the sprawling rooftops, and rested on the grand manor towering above the city center. Resplendent amid the storm-tossed ruins, it was the residence of Huating's Chief of Records.\n\nSuoming nodded quietly to herself. The path she had spent years planning had finally taken its first step.",
+  "FavorStory_131201_Title": "The Return",
+  "FavorStory_131202_Content": "Suoming knew taking Huating by force and striking down its Chief of Records would be easy. But the city's power play ran deep and tangled. Eliminating the chief would not bring true peace, nor would it win the trust of the people. To destroy his hold, she had to dismantle the pillars supporting his authority.\n\nThe first was the city's most arrogant swordsman. Hired at great expense by the Chief of Records, his formidable Forte allowed his blade to slice through iron like mud. Relying on that reputation, he gathered a gang of his own. Many who despised the Chief of Records still trembled before his steel.\n\nWhen Suoming tracked him to a tavern, his men snickered as they let the petite young woman upstairs: \"What does a kid know about drinking?\"\n\nMoments later, the second-story window shattered. A single sword hurtled downward, embedding itself in the street below. The gang fell dead silent as they recalled a rumor from the West Gate: the leader who had broken into Huating was a short girl with red hair.\n\nSobered instantly, the swordsman picked himself up and clashed with Suoming for dozens of rounds before collapsing in exhaustion. Suoming looked down at him. \"With skill like yours, why serve evil? Come with me, and we'll wipe out the Evil Miasma.\"\n\nThe swordsman would go on to become the Third Seal. He later admitted that Suoming's grand ambitions had meant little to him at first. What won him over was her willingness to wield absolute power without ever bullying the weak, and the shame that realization stirred in him.\n\nWith the chief's blade dealt with, Suoming turned her sights toward the city's other power—its wealthiest merchant clan. For generations, they had amassed inexhaustible fortunes in gold, jade, and brocade. Suoming intended to request their financial support, but upon her visit, she met only the clan’s young lady. The girl asked hesitant questions about the outside world, hiding a secret too heavy to speak aloud.\n\nThe First Seal sensed something amiss and advised caution. But when the clan head openly boasted that the Evil Miasma was an opportunity to hoard goods and inflate prices rather than fight, Suoming realized words were pointless. That night, she slipped into the estate and brought the young lady out, learning the truth from her own lips.\n\nThe young lady possessed a Forte that could replicate any object, which is in fact, the true secret behind her family's vast fortune. In return, they had kept her imprisoned in the inner halls year after year, forcing her to copy every treasure brought before her. Once the Evil Miasma isolated the city, she became the clan's source of endless treasures. However, years of captivity and constant depletion of her Forte had left her spirit withered.\n\nSuoming told her plainly: \"I can free you, but beyond freedom, I have nothing to give.\" The young lady followed without a second thought.\n\nYears later, whenever the Fourth Seal combed Suoming's long hair, Suoming would often sigh: \"I should have simply freed you from that mansion and left it at that. I never should have dragged you into this war.\"\n\nThe Fourth Seal would only shake her head, gather up Suoming's red hair, and hum a quiet verse of her own making. It's her gentle way of giving thanks.",
+  "FavorStory_131202_Title": "The Covenant",
+  "FavorStory_131203_Content": "\"How many years has it been since you came to me for the Thousandweight Pin? You kept your word. You brought our people back to Huating and tore down that accursed regime. When the gates opened and the crowds flooded in, the city didn't collapse into chaos. It took on new life. Every bit of it was bought with you and your followers.\"\n\n\"Huating hangs on your every word now. People placed faith in your strength. If you raised a single hand, the whole city could march out and wipe the Evil Miasma away forever.\"\n\n\"But the people behind these walls haven't seen the true calamity. They've never watched black rain drown the soil and wither the earth. They've never seen a loved one eroded past recognition. We have. When I built that pin for you, my only goal was to bring the stranded home. As for defeating the Evil Miasma... I never thought it possible. So don't try to talk me into this again. I won't waste what little strength I have left on a futile cause. And I suggest you... stop chasing it, too.\"\n\nSuoming looked down at the cup of clear local white tea before her. At the bottom, the emblem of the <te href=850085>Ministry of Development</te> rippled faintly in the brew.\n\n\"Huating today isn't my doing alone. The Sixth Seal reads people well and is eloquent beyond measure. It was she who walked the streets, maneuvering deftly among the city's competing factions to earn the people's trust for me. Without her, Huating might never have believed.\"\n\n\"As for the Evil Miasma... there is a physician who, too, has witnessed firsthand the Blight Rain that gnaws bone and the Miasmic Thunder that devours men. Had she surrendered to grief, she would never have probed the nature of the Evil Miasma, nor found a way to combat it. The Fifth Seal has designed a mask capable of resisting the  erosion. It awaits only a master craftsman to forge it. Once complete, the Fourth Seal can replicate as many as we require. With those, we will finally be equipped to step into the Blight Rain. But that alone is not enough. I need you to forge a weapon...\"\n\nThe old man leaned in, waiting for her to continue.\n\n\"I will claim its power. In battle, I will weaken the Evil Miasma. Even if I can seize only a wisp of Blight Rain or a thread of Miasmic Thunder, I will seal it within the weapon. My body may have its limits, but I will master its power and find a way to conquer it.\"\n\n\"Then... what kind of weapon do you envision? A sword? A broadblade? With internal mechanisms, it could—\"\n\n\"An umbrella would be best.\"\n\n\"An umbrella...?\" He turned toward the window, gazing into the distance. \"I see. Against the Blight Rain that fills the heavens, there is indeed no finer shield than an umbrella to shelter Huating from the storm.\"\n\nHe turned back to Suoming.\n\n\"Very well... by your own ranking, where do I stand now? Am I the Seventh Seal?\"\n\nSuoming finally raised her teacup, a rare smile gracing her lips.\n\n\"I have been holding that place for you all along, Second Seal.\"",
+  "FavorStory_131203_Title": "The Umbrella",
+  "FavorStory_131204_Content": "Not long after the Eighth Seal joined the Covenant, the Vermillion Locks were finally complete. Suoming intended to use them to borrow the other nine Seals' abilities against the Evil Miasma. Yet, having never attempted such a feat, even her calm expression hid a trace of hesitation. For a brief moment, she pondered who should step forward first.\n\nIt was the Seventh Seal who volunteered. As a soldier of the <te href=260010>Ministry of War</te>, he insisted it was only right that he take the lead.\n\nWhen Suoming had originally ousted Huating's Chief of Records, the Ministry of War was freed from its shackles. Most of its soldiers eagerly pledged their support, save for the Seventh Seal, who stubbornly refused to yield. To him, no matter how heinous the chief's crimes, he remained Huating's legitimate authority. Suoming, a mere commoner, had no right to replace him. In those early days, the Seventh Seal opposed her at every turn.\n\nIt was only when Suoming escorted him beyond Huating's walls to witness the villages buried beneath the Blight Rain that his resolve shattered. Standing silent among the ruins, he asked her with reddened eyes: \"Could Huating truly be spared such tragedy from now on?\"\n\nThe answer needed no words. From that day forward, the Seventh Seal remained the same steadfast, rule-abiding soldier. He shouldered every burden while insisting that, regardless of Suoming's standing, she was still a citizen of Huating, and it was his duty to protect her.\n\nAccepting his goodwill, Suoming set about sealing a fragment of his frequency into her lock. Moments later, a wisp of mist materialized in her palm—faint, but unmistakably the Seventh Seal's Forte. Simultaneously, the umbrella resting beside her violently shuddered. With a deafening roar, a bolt of crimson-black thunder burst from the umbrella, shattering the courtyard wall.\n\nAs the others stood in stunned silence, the Eighth Seal approached the rubble, inspecting the Miasmic Thunder still crackling among the debris.\n\n\"Have you ever wielded two powers at once before?\" she asked after a quiet pause.\n\nSuoming shook her head.\n\n\"A fraction of your mind is constantly spent suppressing the Evil Miasma sealed within your umbrella. Borrowing the Seventh Seal's power alongside it likely triggered the instability.\"\n\nThe Eighth Seal had been brought into the fold by the Seventh Seal. Suoming remembered their first meeting, when the Eighth Seal had bypassed formalities to ask for an exhaustive account of every battle she had fought against the Evil Miasma over the years. They spoke for an entire day, emerging only as dusk faded into night. Afterward, Suoming announced to the others that their plan was complete.\n\nAccording to the Eighth Seal's design, one would have to venture directly into the swirling black clouds alongside Suoming. The moment Suoming bound the entity, this ally would drive a dagger charged with the Eighth Seal's Forte into its core, forcing it out of the cloud's protection and into an open battle with them all.\n\nYet every Seal already bore a vital role, and none could be spared for such a perilous gambit.\n\nThey needed someone else. One last person...",
+  "FavorStory_131204_Title": "The Strategy",
+  "FavorStory_131205_Content": "\"And that is how I came to know the Eighth Seal.\" Suoming let out a quiet breath, relaxing into the back of her chair. \"As for the last one... the Ninth Seal...\"\n\nShe paused, taking a moment before continuing. \"Our meeting was rather ordinary. That day, I was walking down the street alone when I spotted a piece of... floating rice cake.\"\n\nAt that, the <te href=850083>Sentinel</te> gave a slight nod. Suoming guessed It might be smiling.\n\n\"She was starving, and using her Forte to stay invisible, she was stealing food from the street vendors. So I stood there and waited. When she drifted past me, I stuck out my foot and tripped her. Ninth tumbled to the ground, her invisibility breaking. She turned around to curse me, but stopped dead in her tracks.\"\n\n\"You frightened her.\"\n\n\"Yes.\" Suoming nodded gently. \"My expression must have been terrible, because I knew she was the one—the Ninth Seal. I would have her draw close to the Evil Miasma to strike the killing blow, and then... she would be discovered and killed on the spot.\"\n\n\"And you told her all of this.\"\n\n\"I did.\" Suoming reached out, her fingers brushing the Vermillion Locks at her side. \"But what good did it do? Ninth loved to trail behind me, calling me 'Sister Suoming' at every turn. Whatever I said, she simply nodded along. She even styled her hair to match mine. Within days, she had stopped fearing me entirely. No matter how I reasoned with her, no matter what I told her, she only smiled... afraid of nothing.\"\n\nSuoming's voice grew softer, fading into silence with a quiet sigh. The Sentinel knew she needed no comfort. Even this grief, she wished to carve deep into her heart. It required no empty words, only a path forward.\n\n\"Suoming, I appreciate everything you have done for Huating. But I have nothing left to reward you with. I must even ask you to keep fighting for <te href=850154>Mengzhou</te>.\"\n\n\"It's fine.\" Suoming lifted her head, the gloom vanishing from her eyes. \"Tell me what you need of me. Tell me how to ensure the Evil Miasma never threatens Mengzhou again.\"\n\n\"...Very well. Take out the Vermillion Locks. I have one final duty to entrust to you.\"",
+  "FavorStory_131205_Title": "The Entrustment",
   "FavorStory_140201_Content": "In any social group, there are those who exude a convincing, reliable aura that convinces people they could achieve anything, despite not having started or done it before. \n\nYangyang is one such person.\n\n\"Yangyang? It seems... She can do everything so well.\" People would always say so. This trust probably stems from their experiences with her. \n\nSince childhood, Yangyang has excelled in her studies, including etiquette, music, literature, mathematics, and martial arts... She always ranked at the top in all subjects and exams every year. Even when encountering new knowledge or difficult problems, Yangyang never showed frustration; rather, she would actively try to solve any problem that came her way.\n\nYangyang was always chosen to give speeches at her school's annual ceremonies. But, like everyone else, she would take a deep breath before speaking, hiding her nerves. Despite having some talent for understanding complex concepts, she lacked true genius. And while others may see her as successful and confident, she struggles just as much with everyday problems.\n\nIn her first swordsmanship lesson, she struggled to control her swings and execute proper techniques. Despite this setback, she resolved to work even harder. For the next two months, she practiced 100 times a day and sought guidance from experienced seniors, humbly acknowledging her weaknesses. She then continued to practice with a reflective mindset, improving upon the deficiencies pointed out by others. Eventually, her muscles adapted to the pain and she could withstand several strikes from an opponent.\n\nAnd in another two months, Yangyang, once again, emerged as the top student in her swordsmanship class.\n\nConcerned individuals tried to console Yangyang, reminding her that life is not perfect and that she should be content with the affirmation and praise she receives. But Yangyang would kindly reject this advice, knowing that her drive came from within and not from seeking validation from others. \n\nWas she giving her all? Was everything done to the best of her ability? These were the questions that consumed her. \n\nWith a smile and a gentle shake of her head, she would reject any advice to take it easy, determined to push herself to perfection with no room for regrets or shame.\n\n\"That's our Yangyang, doing amazing as always. We knew you'd make it!\" \n\nThat was when Yangyang became an <te href=850139>Outrider</te> and successfully helped resolve a regional <te href=850081>TD</te> crisis. People praised her excellence as usual.\n\n<i>\"Yes, I did it this time, but how about next time? There are still so many things I could have done better; so many things I don't know. Perhaps I should go seek advice from senior members of the <te href=850086>Midnight Rangers</te>...\"</i>\n\nBeing consistently reliable is daunting, yet crucial. It takes relentless effort and dedication.\n\nYangyang is determined to give her all to achieve this goal. She strives to be the one others can seek help from anytime, and won't stop until she succeeds.",
   "FavorStory_140201_Title": "The Secret to Excellence",
   "FavorStory_140202_Content": "Most people didn't know that Yangyang had a perfect pitch. \n\nPerhaps it was because how she was always a passive presence, never singing in public or taking the spotlight. She preferred to stay in the background and listen. It seemed like she was born to be a listener.\n\nThat's why Chixia was shocked at Yangyang's ability to accurately hum a melody she had only heard. She couldn't help but exclaim, \"How did you memorize that so fast? And it sounded amazing! Good heavens!\"\n\nSuddenly, everyone remembered Yangyang's other identity, the second daughter of a family of musicians. It would be only unnatural if she didn't understand music theory or know how to sing. \n\nThis revelation resolved some of the previous confusion. That perfect way of dealing with people, that etiquette which seemed faultless to even the most demanding people. Yes, of course, she had to come from such a well-educated family. \n\nHowever, it also begged another question: Why would the daughter of such a renowned family go to the border city alone, at the expense of her otherwise luxurious life and promising career? Was there an underlying reason that compelled her to leave? \n\nIf there really was such a reason, Yangyang never mentioned it to anyone. She never complained or avoided responsibilities, whether when she fulfilled her <te href=850139>Outrider</te> responsibilities, or when she spent time with friends. If one had to say, it was her Outrider companions that bothered her more, because... She was always patiently lending an ear.\n\nMany sought solace in confiding to Yangyang, not seeking solutions but simply venting their stress. She knew this well and remained neutral, empathizing with the narrator's feelings. She listened without interruption, offering guidance when needed before returning to a silent role. With her help, the tangled emotions were untangled unnoticed. These cathartic sessions proved valuable. However, Yangyang's support was not blind agreement; she offered advice prioritizing the speaker's desires.\n\nSoon enough, Chixia told everyone else about Yangyang's amazing talent, sparking playful requests for a song.\nYangyang shyly waved off their request, saying, \"Maybe... I'll sing it for everyone later, when I am ready?\" \n\nYangyang looked sincere, so it seemed not just perfunctory or an excuse. It was truly what she thought from the bottom of her heart. But when would that be? Perhaps she wanted to wait until everyone else who wanted her to hear out their worries had finished talking?\n\nUnderstanding and critiquing performances requires a deep knowledge of music. \nHowever, being skilled in music does not necessitate taking the stage. \n\nSo thought Yangyang, as she again chose to step back, allowing others to shine.",
@@ -60890,7 +61065,7 @@
   "FavorWord_121210_Title": "Ideals",
   "FavorWord_121211_Content": "This pendant was left by my parents. To be honest, I can barely remember their faces anymore. But I remember every birthday, they'd take me up to that mountain temple to pray. The place used to be thick with incense smoke. Even in the dead of winter, the place was gilded in warm gold. They'd place this pendant bearing my name before the altar, heads bowed, all faithful, praying to whatever was listening. These days, I no longer believe in some higher powers upstairs or all that destiny nonsense. And it's not the fortune sticks or the wishes I cling to. It's the sunlight after snow... and the sight of them walking by each side of me, their hands holding mine.",
   "FavorWord_121211_Title": "Chat: I",
-  "FavorWord_121212_Content": "Want to know the story behind these pupils? The green one's the original. The gold one? That came after my Forte awakened. Took me a while to stop flinching at this new look when I catch myself in mirror, not gonna lie. Turns out, it lets me see the scattered frequencies in shadowy corners of the world. Kinda like those \"Yin-Yang\" eyes people talk about. Come to think of it... your eyes are gold too, but yours don't look like anyone else's. On you... they look perfectly golden.",
+  "FavorWord_121212_Content": "Want to know the story behind these pupils? The green one's the original. The gold one? That came after my Forte awakened. Took me a while to stop flinching at this new look when I caught myself in mirror, not gonna lie. Turns out, it lets me see the scattered frequencies in shadowy corners of the world. Kinda like those \"Yin-Yang\" eyes people talk about. Come to think of it... your eyes are gold too, but yours don't look like anyone else's. On you... they look perfectly golden.",
   "FavorWord_121212_Title": "Chat: II",
   "FavorWord_121213_Content": "The famous Madam Chongming Pruner... efficient, sharp-minded, and knows her way around all sorts. The Zhaoming Commerce Guild keeps growing with her at the helm. Rare goods from cities in and even outside of Huanglong keep flowing through her hands. Hm, color me impressed—I've seen my fare share of relics and curios. But if she's got the touch to gather all these lost treasures and put them back on the market... hah, that takes real skill.",
   "FavorWord_121213_Title": "About Suisui",
@@ -60902,7 +61077,7 @@
   "FavorWord_121216_Title": "About Shifu",
   "FavorWord_121217_Content": "Our masters were old acquaintances. When I was a kid, the old man couldn't stop talking about him, \"He's young, but his blade is already incredibly fast.\" I wanted to spar with him every single day because of that. Then, the old man just... stopped talking about him. Shut me down every time I asked. After running into him in Xuanfang Hold, I finally got it—he's a sheathed sword. Hasn't been drawn in years, but its steely edge hums in the dark.",
   "FavorWord_121217_Title": "About Qiuyuan",
-  "FavorWord_121218_Content": "Oh, you mean Sweet Dumpling? Haha, yeah, the lynx spirit trapped in my lamp. I call it whatever comes to my mind in the moment. I called it \"Anonymous\" once, and it went absolutely feral. It was hilarious to watch it lose it. Don't be fooled by what you see, though. It took everything I had to seal it in there.",
+  "FavorWord_121218_Content": "Oh, you mean Sweet Dumpling? Haha, yeah, the lynx spirit trapped in my lamp. I call it whatever comes to my mind in the moment. I called it \"Anonymous\" once, and it went absolutely feral. It was hilarious to watch it lose it. Don't be fooled by what you see, though. Took everything I had to seal it in there.",
   "FavorWord_121218_Title": "About Lynx Spirit",
   "FavorWord_121219_Content": "Happy birthday, {PlayerName}. I never cared much for celebrating my own, but Shifu's old lady and my junior wouldn't let theirs pass without some theater. So every year they rope me into it—hanging lanterns, buying sweets, making longevity noodles... Done it enough times that I've actually gotten decent at the grunt work. So, naturally, the prep is all on me now.\nYou know me. I don't offer incense, don't bow to altars, and I sure don't go asking the divinities for favors. So I can't pray for good fortune on someone else's behalf. Then again... if it's you, I don't reckon there's much point in praying anyway. Haven't seen much that you can't handle.\nSo today, just enjoy being the birthday {Male=boy;Female=girl}. Leave everything else to me.",
   "FavorWord_121219_Title": "Birthday Wishes",
@@ -60922,7 +61097,7 @@
